{"name":"Beach Grass","hex":"#dcddb8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"beach grass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of beachgrass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beach_grass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ammophila (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Ammophila is a genus of flowering plants consisting of two or three very similar species of grasses. The genus name Ammophila originates from the Greek words ἄμμος (ámmos), meaning \"sand\", and φίλος (philos), meaning \"friend\". The common names for the grasses include marram grass, bent grass, and beachgrass. The grasses are found almost exclusively on the first line of coastal dunes. Their extensive system of creeping underground stems or rhizomes allows them to thrive under conditions of shifting sands and high winds, and helps stabilize the dunes and prevent coastal erosion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Helmgras_kijkduin_februari_2005.JPG/330px-Helmgras_kijkduin_februari_2005.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammophila_(plant)","wikidata":"Q21130"},{"title":"Beach","blocks":[{"text":"A beach is a landform alongside a body of water which consists of loose particles. The particles composing a beach are typically made from rock, such as sand, gravel, shingle, pebbles, etc., or biological sources, such as mollusc shells or coralline algae. Sediments settle in different densities and structures, depending on the local wave action and weather, creating different textures, colors and gradients or layers of material."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Beach_at_Fort_Lauderdale.jpg/330px-Beach_at_Fort_Lauderdale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beach","wikidata":"Q40080"},{"title":"Grass","blocks":[{"text":"Grass refers to various families of plants. The three major families of grasslike plants are true grasses (Poaceae), sedges (Cyperaceae), and rushes (Juncaceae). Lawns and pasturelands are typically composed of true grasses, five of which cover 46% of the world's arable land: rice, wheat, maize, barley, and sugar cane."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Poa_annua.jpg/330px-Poa_annua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grass","wikidata":"Q643352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/European_Marram_Grass_%28Ammophila_arenaria%29_rolled_leaf_close-up.jpg/500px-European_Marram_Grass_%28Ammophila_arenaria%29_rolled_leaf_close-up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/European_Marram_Grass_%28Ammophila_arenaria%29_rolled_leaf_close-up.jpg/1280px-European_Marram_Grass_%28Ammophila_arenaria%29_rolled_leaf_close-up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/European_Marram_Grass_%28Ammophila_arenaria%29_rolled_leaf_close-up.jpg/500px-European_Marram_Grass_%28Ammophila_arenaria%29_rolled_leaf_close-up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A single leaf of marram grass, showing the rolled leaf which reduces water loss","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEuropean_Marram_Grass_(Ammophila_arenaria)_rolled_leaf_close-up.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg/500px-Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg/1280px-Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnai